= Unit 2 · Existence and transcendence

== Lecture 3 · Zorn's lemma and maximal ideals
#focus[Apply Zorn's lemma with explicit chain upper bounds, prove maximal-ideal existence, and separate algebraic from set-theoretic assumptions.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall maximal means no larger admissible object, whereas greatest means above every object.


Some important existence statements cannot be proved by choosing an element of smallest size. Zorn's lemma replaces a size-minimization argument by an order-theoretic condition on chains. We use the usual axiom of choice in this course through the following equivalent formulation.

#strategy[Specify the poset and verify that a chain union remains admissible before invoking maximality.]

#theorem[Zorn's lemma, foundational assumption][A nonempty partially ordered set in which every chain has an upper bound has a maximal element. A chain is a subset whose elements are pairwise comparable. A maximal element need not be a greatest element.]

The lemma is a set-theoretic principle used here, not a theorem derived from the ring axioms. The mathematical work in an application is to choose the correct poset and verify the upper-bound condition without accidentally including a forbidden object.

#theorem[Existence of maximal ideals][Every nonzero commutative unital ring has a maximal ideal. More generally every proper ideal is contained in a maximal ideal.]
#proof[Fix a proper ideal $I$ and order the proper ideals containing it by inclusion. The poset is nonempty. For a nonempty chain, its union is an ideal: any two elements lie together in one chain member, so their difference lies there, and multiplication by a ring element stays in that member. The union is proper, because if it contained one, one chain member would contain one and would be the whole ring. Thus the union is an upper bound in the poset; the empty chain has upper bound $I$. Zorn supplies a maximal member, which is a maximal ideal of the ring.]

#example[What the proof does and does not give][In $ZZ$, maximal ideals are $(p)$ for primes $p$. In an arbitrary ring, the argument proves existence without an algorithm for finding a maximal ideal. The requirement $1!=0$ matters: the zero ring has no proper ideal.]

#theorem[Vector-space bases][Every vector space has a basis, assuming Zorn's lemma.]
#proof[Order linearly independent subsets by inclusion. Their chain unions are independent because every finite linear dependence would involve elements lying together in one chain member. A maximal independent subset exists. If it did not span, adjoining a vector outside its span would preserve independence, contradicting maximality.]

#warning[For arbitrary collections of ideals, the union need not be an ideal. Comparability in a chain is what places two selected elements in a common ideal. Similarly, independence is preserved by chain unions because every relation is finite.]
#example[The chain condition does the work][The ideals $2ZZ$ and $3ZZ$ are proper, but their union is not an ideal: it contains two and three without containing their sum five. In a chain, one of two selected ideals contains the other, so two chosen elements do lie in a common member and can be added there. Properness uses a different argument: if one belonged to the union, it would belong to a single member.]

These are two independent checks. Closure proves the union is an ideal; the identity test proves it is still in the poset of proper ideals. Omitting either leaves a gap in the Zorn argument.

#checkpoint("2.1", [Where does the identity element enter the maximal-ideal proof?], [It detects properness: an ideal is the whole ring exactly when it contains one, so a union containing one would force one chain member to be improper.])
#takeaways[Every Zorn proof needs four visible ingredients: the poset, nonemptiness, chain upper bounds, and the implication of maximality.]
