== Lecture 4 · Algebraic closures and transcendence bases
#focus[Construct an algebraic closure and prove existence of a transcendence basis. Recall algebraic extensions, polynomial rings, and finite dependence.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall an extension generated by finitely many algebraic elements is finite.


#definition[Algebraic closure][An algebraic closure of $K$ is an algebraic extension $overline(K)/K$ in which every nonconstant polynomial has a root. Repeatedly factoring off roots then makes every polynomial split.]

#theorem[Existence of algebraic closures][Every field has an algebraic closure.]
#strategy[Adjoin roots of all current polynomials at once, then repeat countably many times so that polynomials involving new coefficients also acquire roots.]
#proof[For each monic nonconstant $f in K[x]$, introduce a variable $X_f$. In the polynomial ring on all these variables, the ideal generated by the elements $f(X_f)$ is proper. Otherwise an expression for one would use finitely many generators; choose a field splitting those finitely many polynomials and evaluate their variables at roots, assigning any other finitely occurring variables arbitrary values. This would send one to zero, impossible. Extend the ideal to a maximal ideal and take the field quotient. The base field embeds because the quotient is nonzero. Every generator image is algebraic, so every element of this field is algebraic over $K$, since it uses finitely many algebraic generators. Call it $K_1$. Repeat to get $K subset.eq K_1 subset.eq K_2 subset.eq dots$. The union is a field and is algebraic over $K$ by transitivity of algebraicity. A polynomial over the union has finitely many coefficients, all in some $K_n$, and therefore has a root in $K_(n+1)$. The union is an algebraic closure.]

#definition[Algebraic independence][A subset $B subset.eq L$ is algebraically independent over $K$ if no nonzero polynomial over $K$ in finitely many distinct elements of $B$ evaluates to zero. A transcendence basis is such a $B$ for which $L/K(B)$ is algebraic.]

#theorem[Existence of transcendence bases][Every field extension $L/K$ has a transcendence basis.]
#proof[Order the algebraically independent subsets by inclusion. The empty set qualifies, and a chain union is independent because a polynomial relation uses only finitely many elements, which occur together in one chain member. Zorn gives a maximal independent set $B$. If $alpha in L$ were transcendental over $K(B)$, then $B union\{alpha\}$ would be independent: a polynomial relation, viewed as a polynomial in $alpha$, has coefficients that are polynomials in finitely many elements of $B$, not all zero by independence. It would make $alpha$ algebraic over $K(B)$, a contradiction. Thus $L/K(B)$ is algebraic.]

#example[Rational functions][$\{t_1,dots,t_n\}$ is a transcendence basis of $K(t_1,dots,t_n)$ over $K$. Adjoining $sqrt(t_1)$ in characteristic not two gives an algebraic extension without changing that chosen transcendence basis. A transcendence basis need not generate the whole field as a field.]
#example[A transcendence basis need not generate the field][In $K(t)$, the singleton $\{t^2\}$ is algebraically independent, since a nonzero polynomial in $t^2$ would produce a nonzero polynomial in the indeterminate $t$. Yet $t$ satisfies $X^2-t^2$ over $K(t^2)$, so $K(t)$ is algebraic over that subfield. Thus $\{t^2\}$ is a transcendence basis even though adjoining it as a rational-function generator need not recover all of $K(t)$.]

The definition has two clauses: independence over the original base and algebraicity over the generated field. Maximality supplies the second only after proving that an element transcendental over that field could be adjoined without destroying independence.

#checkpoint("2.2", [Why is adjoining roots only once insufficient for an algebraic closure?], [New elements become coefficients of new polynomials. A field containing roots of all polynomials over the original base need not yet contain roots of all polynomials over itself.])
#takeaways[The common mechanism is finite dependence inside a maximal or increasing construction. Keep it visible in both existence proofs.]
